WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.220
This video shows how to protect a single virtual machine with a Kube-OVN security group. Where an access control list applies to a whole subnet, a security group follows one network port.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:20.680
Kube-OVN security groups have no menu entry or form yet, but the UI lists them on their resource page, kubeovn.io.securitygroup, and creates them from YAML.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.580
Select Create from YAML.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:40.560
Paste the security group demo-db-sg. Its one ingress rule allows TCP port 5432 from the app subnet, 10.230.2.0/24. Everything else coming in is denied. The egress rule lets the machine answer and reach out.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.900
Select Create.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.710
The security group exists, but no port uses it yet.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:52.670
Bind it to the database virtual machine. Open the action menu of demo-db,

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.440
and select Edit YAML.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:03.910
Kube-OVN reads per-port settings from annotations on the virtual machine template. This one already pins the address on the demo-db-net network.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:12.650
Add two more for the same network: port_security true turns on filtering for the port, and security_groups names demo-db-sg.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.020
Select Save.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:21.500
The port picks up the annotations when the virtual machine starts again, so restart demo-db.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:27.670
Confirm with Apply.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:31.200
demo-db is running again, with the same address.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.310
Open the console of demo-web.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:40.980
Log in as sles, with the password that cloud-init set.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:54.660
Ping demo-db, then request its port 5432.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:59.390
Both fail. The web subnet is not in the security group's rule.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:02.580
Then open the console of demo-app.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.260
Log in the same way.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:17.840
Request port 5432 from the app tier, then ping.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:28.440
The database answers on port 5432, and the ping is dropped. The rule follows demo-db's port, so it would protect the machine on any subnet.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:39.844
The security group protects demo-db's port alone: only the app subnet gets in, and only on port 5432. Next, you steer traffic for a whole VPC with policy routes.
